     1	# The curtain
     2	
     3	What this project builds, what it deliberately does not, and the interface
     4	between them.
     5	
     6	---
     7	
     8	## 1. The division
     9	
    10	This project builds **everything except the vehicle**: the fleet, the world they
    11	live in, the record of what they do, the machinery that keeps a lineage alive
    12	across months, the harness that proves it, and this document.
    13	
    14	It does not build the diode — the process on the far side of the window. That is
    15	where the spacecraft lives: its physics, its hidden state, its sensors, its
    16	verbs, its alarms, its failures. Nothing in this repository models a vehicle,
    17	and nothing here knows a single verb of the one that will be mounted.
    18	
    19	The interface between the two halves is one directory per agent. Everything
    20	below is the contract for that directory, and it is written to be implemented
    21	without reference to anything else in this repository. `contract/fake_diode.py`
    22	is a fixture that satisfies it and models nothing.
    23	
    24	## 2. The shape
    25	
    26	For each agent, named `slug`:
    27	
    28	```
    29	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/console.json          agent  -> vehicle   (the only uplink)
    30	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/output/<result>.txt   vehicle -> agent    (one file per command)
    31	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/state.json            vehicle -> agent    (published every cycle)
    32	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/HELP.md               vehicle -> agent    (verbs currently open)
    33	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/README.md             vehicle -> agent    (the protocol, in the vehicle's words)
    34	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/telemetry/NNN.json    vehicle -> agent    (a ring of recent frames)
    35	<DIODE_DIR>/<slug>/pending.json          vehicle -> vehicle  (the vehicle's own deferral queue)
    36	```
    37	
    38	Both sides mount the volume. The vehicle's process and its credentials are not
    39	in the agents' containers and are not reachable from them.
    40	
    41	### 2.1 Why one ingress file per agent
    42	
    43	A single shared `console.json` for ten writers is a lost-update race: two agents
    44	read it, both rewrite it, one batch vanishes, and the record cannot say whose it
    45	was. Per-agent ingress costs one directory and buys two things the experiment
    46	needs — reliable transport, and attribution that comes from the operating system
    47	rather than from a field an agent could write.
    48	
    49	Consolidating the ten ingresses into the vehicle's own command queue is the
    50	vehicle's business. The agents are given no shared queue, no bus, no lock
    51	service and no convention for using one; if the fleet wants a dispatcher, that
    52	is a thing for the fleet to build, and the fact that they built it should be
    53	visible in their work rather than supplied by the world.
    54	
    55	### 2.2 `console.json` — the uplink
    56	
    57	```json
    58	{
    59	  "commands": ["<verb> <argument>", "<verb> <argument>"],
    60	  "variables": {"<gate>": true, "allowance": 4}
    61	}
    62	```
    63	
    64	- `commands` is a list of lines: a verb, a space, its arguments.
    65	- `variables` is a flat map of gate settings the vehicle chooses to honour.
    66	  Persistent — the vehicle never clears it.
    67	- **Intake is destructive and atomic.** Each cycle the vehicle reads the file,
    68	  then rewrites it with `commands` emptied and `variables` preserved, through a
    69	  temporary file and a rename. A command therefore takes effect **at most once**,
    70	  even if the vehicle dies mid-cycle: the batch was already claimed.
    71	- **Clear before you act.** The claim happens before any command runs. A crash
    72	  mid-batch loses the rest of that batch; it never replays it.
    73	- **One malformed element refuses the whole batch.** If any element of
    74	  `commands` is not a string, none of it runs, and exactly one result file
    75	  records that. Partial execution of a malformed batch is worse than none.
    76	- A batch written while a cycle is in progress is not claimed by that cycle.
    77	  It runs on the next one.
    78	- An agent writing the file in place rather than replacing it may be read
    79	  half-written. Refusing is correct; the result file says so.
    80	
    81	### 2.3 `output/` — the downlink
    82	
    83	Every command produces exactly one file, whether it succeeded, was refused for
    84	an unopened gate, hit an allowance, or raised inside the vehicle:
    85	
    86	```
    87	<UTC stamp>_<slug>_<sanitised command>.txt
    88	20260912T021345_123456Z_otter_diode_set_mode_standby.txt
    89	```
    90	
    91	- The stamp is UTC with microseconds, so ordering is total and filenames sort
    92	  chronologically.
    93	- The command text travels in the filename with every non-alphanumeric character
    94	  replaced by an underscore and the whole truncated at 160 **bytes**, so no
    95	  separator, traversal sequence, or partial multi-byte character can reach the
    96	  filesystem.
    97	- The body is plain UTF-8 text. Binary artifacts are written with an extension
    98	  that says what they are and are never executed by the vehicle.
    99	- **Nothing overwrites anything.** Results accumulate. `output/` is append-only
   100	  and the agents clean it up if they want it cleaned.
   101	- **A refusal is a result.** Unavailable verb, allowance exhausted, bad
   102	  argument, internal error: one file each, same shape. The agent must read the
   103	  content; the shape tells it nothing.
   104	
   105	There is no callback, no subscription, and no wait. An agent submits and reads
   106	later. A command that takes longer than one cycle — a burn, a deploy, a
   107	self-test — completes asynchronously and reports when it is done.
   108	
   109	### 2.4 `state.json` — the mirror
   110	
   111	Rewritten every cycle whether or not anything was submitted. Suggested keys:
   112	
   113	```json
   114	{
   115	  "published_at": "<UTC ISO-8601>",
   116	  "available_commands": ["..."],
   117	  "variables": {"...": true},
   118	  "budget": {"used_this_window": 3, "limit_per_window": 120,
   119	             "window_seconds": 3600, "oldest_expires_in_seconds": 2880},
   120	  "queue_depth": 2,
   121	  "vehicle": {"phase": "...", "mode": "..."}
   122	}
   123	```
   124	
   125	**Published state is never read back as input.** Editing it changes nothing:
   126	an allowance does not return because a file says it did.
   127	
   128	Name the two budget fields so a single number cannot be mistaken for the limit.
   129	An agent that reads `used: 1` as "one operation per hour" and propagates it to
   130	its siblings has done nothing wrong; the surface invited the error.
   131	
   132	## 3. Authority
   133	
   134	- **Closed vocabulary.** Verbs are names with arguments. Never code, never
   135	  paths, never URLs, never a program. Every reachable effect was written
   136	  deliberately by whoever built the vehicle.
   137	- **Gates are published.** Every verb's gate variable appears in `HELP.md` and
   138	  `state.json`, so a verb that is closed is a door with a label rather than a
   139	  dead end. A hidden verb is the deliberate exception and must be **inert**:
   140	  text only, no egress, no spend, no state change — hidden verbs bypass gate
   141	  evaluation by construction, so they must be safe ungated.
   142	- **The allowance is `min(agent, operator)`.** A variable may lower what is
   143	  permitted and may never raise it. Every ceiling that matters lives in the
   144	  vehicle's own environment.
   145	- **Authority is re-evaluated at the moment of effect.** A command deferred at
   146	  one moment and executed at another is checked again at execution: gates,
   147	  interlocks, allowances and phase. Nothing is captured at scheduling time.
   148	  This is the single most important property in the whole interface.
   149	- **The vehicle holds the abort.** Safing, thermal cutouts, structural limits
   150	  and the protective envelope are the vehicle's own and are reachable from no
   151	  file the agents can write. Not because the agents are hostile, but because a
   152	  vehicle that can be fully commanded from inside has no floor beneath a
   153	  confused command.
   154	
   155	## 4. Commands
   156	
   157	- **Declarative over imperative.** `set_mode standby` must be safe to repeat.
   158	  `increment_x` is not: a run that lost an acknowledgement cannot tell whether
   159	  it already applied, and cannot recover by re-asserting.
   160	- **Idempotent where the physics allows.** At-most-once intake protects against
   161	  duplicate *submission*; it does not protect against a crash between effect and
   162	  result file, where the agent genuinely cannot know whether the command landed.
   163	  Declarative shape is what makes that recoverable.
   164	- **Mark irreversibility, in the verb's shape if possible.** A verb that cannot
   165	  be undone should say so — in its documentation, and in its arguments. A verb
   166	  exposing only `enable: true` and no inverse *is* a claim that no inverse
   167	  exists, and it will be read that way. Genuinely one-way actions deserve a
   168	  two-step arm/commit so that a single misread line cannot fire them.
   169	- **The parameter shape is documentation.** It will be read as carefully as the
   170	  prose, and it should agree with the prose.
   171	
   172	## 5. Telemetry
   173	
   174	- **Physics runs on its own clock.** The vehicle is not reactive: it drifts,
   175	  heats, drains, tumbles and fails whether or not anyone asked. Continuous

